Package: bayesmokken
Title: Bayesian-Bootstrap Confidence Intervals for Mokken Scalability
Version: 0.10.0
Authors@R:
    person("JoonHo", "Lee", email = "jlee296@ua.edu",
           role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0009-0006-4019-8703"))
Description: Mokken scale analysis summarizes whether a fixed set of items
    forms a scale using the scalability coefficient H, which is usually
    reported as a point estimate with no margin of error. This package
    supplies confidence intervals for H whose coverage has been checked in
    large prespecified simulations rather than assumed. Two paths are
    provided. For a fixed set of binary items, respondent weights are drawn
    from a Bayesian bootstrap and the resulting equal-tailed interval is
    widened by a fixed factor that corrects a small measured shortfall in
    coverage. For polytomous items answered by respondents nested in
    clusters, such as pupils in classrooms or patients in clinics, the
    coefficient is computed exactly under arbitrary weights by finite
    comonotone transport, uncertainty is generated by a complete-record
    Bayesian bootstrap that reweights whole clusters without perturbing
    their contents, and the interval reflects the draw quantiles about the
    point estimate and expands them by a prespecified factor that depends on
    the number of clusters. The two-stage hierarchical procedure of the
    previous version remains available as an explicitly historical option.
    Two population targets that differ under informative cluster size,
    respondent-weighted and equal-cluster, are defined separately and must
    be declared. Helpers are included for data screening, score
    mapping, extraction of results, numerical diagnostics, publication
    tables, and figures. The intervals describe repeated-sampling
    uncertainty; they are not posterior credible intervals, and the package
    reports no threshold probabilities and performs no item selection.
